Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Asymmetric progressive limb weakness, Upper and lower motor neuron signs, Muscle fasciculations/atrophy, Bulbar involvement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-002
Reason for Visit: Acute distress following a choking episode and progressive functional decline.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver (daughter)
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a recent unprovoked choking episode on saliva, coupled with acute-on-chronic weakness leading to a near-fall. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the immediate risk of aspiration, respiratory compromise, and need for urgent safety optimization.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I couldn't stop coughing after swallowing, and my legs just gave out on me today.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-week history of asymmetric progressive distal limb weakness, which worsened abruptly today causing frequent tripping. Two hours ago, the patient experienced a severe choking episode on their own secretions due to established bulbar involvement, resulting in prolonged coughing and distress. The caregiver notes new visible muscle fasciculations in the proximal limbs and tongue over the last 48 hours. The patient has had preserved sphincter control but reports increasing anxiety regarding respiratory muscle weakness.
Functional Status: Requires assistance for safe ambulation; dependent for meal preparation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver fatigue and high risk of aspiration and falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 65.0 Kg
General: Patient appears fatigued and anxious, with a weak, wet-sounding voice.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals profound muscle atrophy in the hands and intrinsic foot muscles. There are widespread visible muscle fasciculations, prominently in the tongue and bilateral deltoids. Upper motor neuron signs include hyperreflexia in the lower limbs and a positive Babinski sign on the right. Lower motor neuron signs include hyporeflexia and flaccid weakness in the upper limbs. Bulbar assessment shows dysarthria and pooling of oral secretions. Respiratory exam shows clear lung fields but shallow diaphragmatic excursion.
Environmental Observations: Narrow doorways hindering walker use; thick liquids available but not being used.
Medication Reconciliation: Riluzole dosing confirmed; PRN hyoscine patch applied correctly.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (with acute bulbar decompensation and falls risk) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to the chronic, progressive nature of the asymmetric weakness, presence of widespread fasciculations, and lack of sudden unilateral dense hemiparesis or visual field deficits.

Myasthenia Gravis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of prominent upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia, Babinski) and lack of fluctuating ocular symptoms (ptosis, diplopia).

Cervical Myelopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to the prominent bulbar involvement (dysarthria, tongue fasciculations) which localizes pathology above the cervical spine.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute choking episode and functional decline driven by progressive bulbar and motor neuron dysfunction. The objective findings of resting tachypnea, pooling secretions, and mixed upper/lower motor neuron signs with a recent near-fall required urgent evaluation. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because the immediate risk of aspiration pneumonia and major traumatic injury from unmitigated falls necessitated rapid in-home clinical stabilization and caregiver education on secretion management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered sublingual atropine drops to manage acute sialorrhea. Educated on positional feeding and chin-tuck maneuvers.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you utilize the chin-tuck posture for every swallow, you protect your airway from fluid; therefore, practice this specific posture with empty swallows to build muscle memory and confidence. When you ring the bell for assistance before standing, you avoid falls; rewarding yourself with a safe, guided walk reinforces asking for help.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community speech pathology and occupational therapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): Prescription for a customized shower chair and thickened fluid trial.
Follow-up: Routine GP review early next week for advanced care planning and symptom review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately if there is a choking episode that does not clear within 1 minute, if the patient turns blue, or if severe shortness of breath develops while resting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of acute aspiration is clinically ruled out for now, but conditions are dynamic and risk remains high.
Clinical Course: The condition is progressively neurodegenerative; acute secretion management will temporarily improve comfort, but swallowing difficulty will persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek urgent medical review if new fever, productive cough, or increasing work of breathing occurs over the next 24 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The atropine drops will dry up the excess saliva to prevent choking. The chin-tuck maneuver mechanically narrows the airway opening during swallowing.
Actionable Steps: Use thickened fluids only. Perform the chin-tuck maneuver. Always call for assistance before mobilizing.

